From 55038da45ba957832fe6565cfce692ea606ed601 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roi Blum <roiblum05@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:49:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] feat: keep Agents carrying a label out of a NodePool
 (excludeAgentLabels)

excludeAgentLabels, a list of label keys set in any values file (the most
specific file that sets it wins, as Helm replaces a list), and a nodepool's own
excludeAgentLabels, added to it, each render as a
{key: <key>, operator: DoesNotExist} requirement in the NodePool's
agentLabelSelector: next to its matchLabels (one InfraEnv) or after the In
requirement (several, additionalInfraEnvNames), sorted and unique. A NodePool
then never takes an Agent that carries the label.

The use is herzi's collector of used Agents: herzi labels every Agent
installed in a cluster as used, since an uninstall or a scale-down does not
return a used Agent to the inventory cleanly, and deletes a free Agent that
carries the label. Every NodePool excluding it keeps a freed used Agent from
being bound again before herzi deletes it. The label's name is herzi's to
choose, so the value is a generic list of keys.

Unset or empty, the render is byte for byte what it was: the golden tests
pass, and a test renders every fixture with an empty list and compares the
output. Agent platform only: a KubeVirt nodepool skips the chart-wide list,
and its own list fails the render. The render also fails on a key that is not
a label key and on the label the NodePool selects its InfraEnv by.

The README says what a changed selector does: nothing to running nodes on an
InPlace NodePool, every node replaced on a Replace one; and that herzi on its
main reports a NodePool with matchExpressions beside its matchLabels as a
problem of the InfraEnv's pool, which then orders no servers for any cluster,
so an exclusion waits for the herzi that accepts it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016gPPBP4UxCerc81DKaJHKm
